# Query 7: Cartesian Product with GENOP
# Demonstrates how GENOP handles input variables from multiple BGPs with no shared variables
# 
# This query demonstrates Cartesian Product by:
# 1. BGP1: Querying scientists from the database (binds ?scientist)
# 2. BGP2: Querying awards from the database (binds ?awardLabel)
#    - Note: These two BGPs have NO shared variables, so they produce a Cartesian product
# 3. GENOP: Uses both ?scientist and ?awardLabel as input variables
#    - For each combination of (scientist, award), GENOP generates a description
#
# Key: Since BGP1 and BGP2 have no shared variables, they produce a Cartesian product.
# If BGP1 has 3 scientists and BGP2 has 3 awards, GENOP will be called 3×3=9 times.
#
# Example result combinations:
# - (Albert Einstein, Nobel Prize in Physics) → description
# - (Albert Einstein, Nobel Prize in Chemistry) → description
# - (Albert Einstein, Order of the British Empire) → description
# - (Alan Turing, Nobel Prize in Physics) → description
# - ... (all 9 combinations)

PREFIX ex: <http://example.org/>
PREFIX foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/>
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>

SELECT ?scientist ?awardLabel ?description WHERE {
  # BGP1: Query scientists (binds ?scientist only)
  ?person foaf:name ?scientist .
  
  # BGP2: Query awards (binds ?awardLabel only)
  # Note: No shared variables with BGP1 → Cartesian product
  ?award rdfs:label ?awardLabel .
  
  # GENOP: Uses both ?scientist and ?awardLabel as input variables
  # This will be called for each combination from the Cartesian product
  GENOP("Write a one-sentence description about {?scientist} and their relationship to {?awardLabel}. Be creative and interesting.",
        ?description,
        <model:openrouter:xiaomi/mimo-v2-flash:free>)
}

